  "Number": 5,
  "Question": "If \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) are constants and \\[(x+2)(x+b)=x^2+cx+6,\\] then \\(c\\) is",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-5\\)",
    "B": "\\(-3\\)",
    "C": "\\(-1\\)",
    "D": "\\(3\\)",
    "E": "\\(5\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Expanding the product reveals how \\(b\\) determines the constant term and the coefficient of \\(x\\):\n\\[\n(x+2)(x+b)=x^2+(b+2)x+2b.\n\\]\nBecause the given equality holds for every \\(x\\), the coefficients of corresponding powers of \\(x\\) must agree. Comparing constant terms gives \\(2b=6\\), so \\(b=3\\). Comparing coefficients of \\(x\\) then gives\n\\[\nc=b+2=3+2=5.\n\\]\nIndeed, \\((x+2)(x+3)=x^2+5x+6\\), as required. Thus \\(\\boxed{c=5}\\), choice \\(\\text{E}\\).",
